course: dictionary
lesson: 1081
title: Boycott: saying no together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A boycott is a peaceful way to protest. To boycott something means you refuse to buy it, use it, or take part in it, to show you think it is wrong. A boycott can also be the organised refusal itself, as in «The bus boycott that followed helped end segregation laws.»
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. «Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat» tells you that she said no. «The bus boycott that followed» tells you that many people then said no together. Both words are about refusing, but a boycott is a refusal that many people organise.
explain: Boycott is a verb and a noun. You can say, «People began to boycott the buses,» or «People joined the boycott.» Both sentences are about refusing, and the word boycott works in each. Try swapping in «refuse to use» and see whether the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Boycott means to refuse to buy, use, or take part in something, as a protest.
fact: A boycott is an organised refusal of this kind, done by many people together.
fact: «The Montgomery bus boycott» was led by Martin Luther King and others, after Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat in Alabama.
fact: The boycott helped end segregation laws, which kept Black and white people apart on buses, in schools and in shops.
fact: Boycotts are peaceful protests. Countries sometimes boycott the Olympics, and people boycott companies that harm workers or the planet.
exercise: «The bus boycott that followed helped end segregation laws.» What is a boycott? | A prize for the best bus driver | A new bus with more seats | A race to see who can get on the bus first | *A peaceful refusal to use something, as a protest
exercise: «In 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat in Alabama.» Find and copy one word that means said no. | *refused | bus | seat | Alabama
exercise: A shop treats its workers unfairly. What could people do as a peaceful protest? | *Stop buying from that shop | Shout at the shop owner in the street | Break the shop windows | Ask the shop to close for a holiday
test: «Many ordinary people, including women, joined the boycott.» What does boycott mean here? | *Refusing to buy or use something, as a protest | Running in a race | Painting a sign for a shop | Buying as much as you can
test: They decided to ___ the shop until it treated its workers fairly. | build | celebrate | sing | *boycott
exam: Which is the best reason a person might boycott a company? | It is near the train station | It sells very cheap bread | Its shop has a blue door | *It harms workers or the planet
